    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d770">

                <pb facs="0949" n="935" sameAs="frus:frus1945v09/pg935"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.61351/6–2245: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Cuba</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Wright</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Habana</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-06-22T19:00:00-04:00">June 22, 1945—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 8:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>424. This morning at final meeting with Cuban Sugar Commission,<anchor n="83"
                        xml:id="d770-f1"/> they stated that because of 1946 crop uncertainty and our
                    offer for only one crop, they could not get industry’s support to continue
                    negotiations until October. They, however, repeatedly assured that U.S. would
                    get Cuba’s 1946 production.</p>
                <pb facs="0950" n="936" xml:id="pg936"/>
                <p>A joint press release was issued stating our offer to purchase one crop at 3.45
                    and that discussions would be resumed with Cubans before October 15.</p>
                <p>Wilson and Rowe left for Washington this afternoon.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wright</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d770-f1" n="83" xml:id="d770-f1-content">
                <p>Meetings of the United States and Cuban Commissions began in Habana on June
                    10.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
